  "result": "Fixed. The quote now prints 27.06 and all 6 tests pass.\n\n**Root cause:** `to_cents` in `shop/money.py:9` padded the fractional part with `rjust`, which adds zeros on the left. A one-digit fraction like \"5\" became \"05\", so \"12.5\" parsed as 1205 cents instead of 1250. Two units at 1205 plus 8.25% tax rounds to 2609, which is the 26.09 you saw. Prices with two decimals were unaffected, which is why nothing in the existing suite caught it.\n\n**Fix:** changed the padding to `ljust`, so \"5\" becomes \"50\". Two-digit fractions, bare integers, and a trailing dot all still parse correctly.\n\n**Verification:**\n- Test suite via the standard library runner: 6 tests, all OK. Pytest is not installed in this environment.\n- The original command prints 27.06.\n- Spot-checked \"12.5\", \"12.50\", \"12.05\", \"12\", \"12.\", \"0.5\", \"0.05\", and \"1.99\" against expected cent values.\n\nThe suite has no test for `to_cents` itself, so a one-line regression test for a single-digit fraction would be a worthwhile follow-up.",
